-artificial-intelligence-bias">What is Artificial Intelligence Bias</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/who-is-the-father-of-artificial-intelligence">Who is the Father of Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/sop-for-ms-in-artificial-intelligence">SOP for MS in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/top-ai-penny-stocks-in-india">Top AI Penny Stocks in India</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-ai-in-data-analytics">What is AI in Data Analytics</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-business-management">Artificial Intelligence in Business Management</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/partial-order-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Partial Order Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-auditing">Artificial Intelligence in Auditing</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-construction">Artificial Intelligence in Construction</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/information-retrieval-in-artificial-intelligence">Information Retrieval in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/artificial-intelligence-in-psychology">Artificial Intelligence in Psychology</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/iterative-deepening-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Iterative Deepening Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/applications-of-deep-learning-in-artificial-intelligence">Applications of Deep Learning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/7-artificial-intelligence-projects-in-agriculture">7 Artificial Intelligence Projects in Agriculture</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/what-is-multimodal-ai">What is Multimodal AI</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/benefits-of-artificial-intelligence-in-business">Benefits of Artificial Intelligence in Business</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conceptual-graphs-in-artificial-intelligence">Conceptual Graphs in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/problem-space-and-search-in-artificial-intelligence">Problem Space and Search in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/conditional-planning-in-artificial-intelligence">Conditional Planning in Artificial Intelligence</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/informed-search-algorithms-in-artificial-intelligence">Informed Search Algorithms in Artificial Intelligence</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="accordion-item" style="background-color:var(--sidebar)"><h2 class="accordion-header" id="flush-heading10"><button aria-controls="flush-collapse10" aria-expanded="true" class="accordion-button sidebar-btn" data-bs-target="#flush-collapse10" data-bs-toggle="collapse" type="button">Related Tutorials</button></h2><div aria-labelledby="flush-heading10" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" id="flush-collapse10"><div class="accordion-body"><ul><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/tensorflow">Tensorflow Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/pytorch">PyTorch Tutorial</a></li><li class="leftmenu"><a href="/reinforcement-learning">Reinforcement Learning</a></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="col-12 col-md-7"><div class="overview"><html><head></head><body><div id="city"><table><tbody><tr><td><div id="bottomnextup"><a class="next" href="artificial-intelligence-in-software-engineering">next →</a> <a class="next" href="artificial-intelligence-in-social-networking">← prev</a></div><h1 class="h1">Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence</h1><div style="font-size:14px;margin-top:5px;margin-bottom:5px;color:#6f6e6e"><span>15 Apr 2025</span> | <span><i aria-hidden="true" class="fa fa-clock-o"></i> 17 min read</span></div><p>Tic Tac Toe is a simple yet interesting game, making it an ideal platform for studying the capabilities of Artificial Intelligence in game theory. The game is simply for two players, played by placing alternately their marks on a 3 x 3 grid with the intention of lining up three of their own marks horizontally, vertically, or diagonally. Even though it seems simple, Tic Tac Toe instigates fundamental concepts in AI, such as choices of decisions, strategy formulation, and opponent prediction; all the more reason it can be a good entrance for all those developers and researchers who want to plunge into the world of AI, game theory, and algorithm development.</p><p>The essence of the Tic Tac Toe problem lies in finding the best strategy that a player could adopt to win or at least not lose. Because there are only a finite number of board states-no more than 9!-it's within the realm of possibility for an AI system to examine all possible outcomes and choose the best move at each turn. This means that the AI must behave like human intelligence, making an effective decision based on the state of the board, the anticipation of the opponent's moves, and an adjustment of the strategy.</p><p><strong>Code:</strong></p><p>Now, for the sake of better understanding, we will implement a model for a play tic tac toe game.</p><h2 class="h2">Importing Libraries</h2><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"># Importing required modules
import numpy as np 
import pandas as pd 
import pprint,random

from scipy.ndimage.interpolation import shift
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

from keras.models import Sequential
from keras.layers import Dense,Dropout
from keras.optimizers import SGD
</textarea></div><h2 class="h2">Creating a Tic-Tac-Toe Game</h2><p>First of all, we would have to implement the game itself to learn how to play Tic-Tac-Toe. One of the most useful libraries of Python for numerical computations is Numpy. We can make a simple implementation of the game quite easily. In this Numpy array, we represent the game's board as a 3x3 Numpy array. It uses a certain type of denotation: 2 stands for a cell with no one, or in other words, for an empty cell, then 0 for 'O', and 1 for 'X'. By default, the array is filled with the number 2. It means that all places on the board are initially empty. In this Numpy array, players take turns choosing coordinates where their respective markers-a 0 or a 1-will go. Quite basically, the win condition for every player in this game is to have three marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line. For simplicity in this implementation, the program will always play with the mark 1 ('X'), and the opponent will play with the mark 0 ('O'). The idea of having such a straightforward approach is to simply focus on the game mechanics and make a setup to teach the program how to strategize and play effectively.</p><p>Below is a diagram to get you familiarized with the Numpy Tic Tac Toe board:</p><img alt="Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/ai/images/tic-tac-toe-problem-in-artificial-intelligence.png"/><br/><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">class game_tic_tac_toe(object):
    def __init__(self):
        self.board=np.full((3,3),2)

    def toss(self):
        """ Function that simulates the tossing of a coin to decide who plays first.

        Args:

        Returns:
        Returns 1 if the player assigned mark 1 has won, or 0 if his opponent won

        """
        turn=np.random.randint(0,2,size=1)
        if turn.mean()==0:
            self.monitor_turn=0
        elif turn.mean()==1:
            self.monitor_turn=1
        return self.monitor_turn

    def move(self,player,coord):
        """ Function to perform the action of making a mark on the tic tac toe board.
After executing the action, this function toggles the value of the monitor_turn to
the next player


        Args:
        player: 1 if the player who is assigned the mark 1 is performing the action, 
        0 if his opponent is performing the action
        coord: The coordinate where the 1 or 0 is to be placed on the 
        tic-tac-toe board (numpy array)

        Returns:
        status_of_game(): Calls the game status function and returns its value
        board: Returns the new board state after making the move

        """
        if self.board[coord]!=2 or self.status_of_game()!="In Progress" or self.monitor_turn!=player:
            raise ValueError("Invalid move")
        self.board[coord]=player
        self.monitor_turn=1-player
        return self.status_of_game(),self.board


    def status_of_game(self):
        """A function to check the current status of the game.
Whether the game is won, drawn, or in progress.


        Args:

        Returns:
        "Won" if the game has been won, "Drawn" if the 
        the game has been drawn, or "In Progress", if the game is still in progress

        """
        #checking for a win along rows
        for i in range(self.board.shape[0]):
            if 2 not in self.board[i,:] and len(set(self.board[i,:]))==1:
                return "Won"
        #checknig for a win along columns
        for j in range(self.board.shape[1]):
            if 2 not in self.board[:,j] and len(set(self.board[:,j]))==1:
                return "Won"
        # check for a win along diagonals
        if 2 not in np.diag(self.board) and len(set(np.diag(self.board)))==1:
            return "Won"
        if 2 not in np.diag(np.fliplr(self.board)) and len(set(np.diag(np.fliplr(self.board))))==1:
            return "Won"
        # checking for a Draw
        if not 2 in self.board:
            return "Drawn"
        else:
            return "In Progress"
</textarea></div><h2 class="h2">Testing Tic-Tac-Toe Game</h2><p>Now we will make some dummy play to test the above-created tic-tac-toe game. We need to make a move for each of the players.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"># create an object of the class tick_tac_toe_game
game=game_tic_tac_toe()
