</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/ai/images/tic-tac-toe-problem-in-artificial-intelligence2.png"/><h2 class="h2">Generating Legal Moves</h2><p>A legal moves generator is a function that receives as input the state of the board and a player, returning the set of all possible legal moves.</p><p>More intuitively, given the state of the board and a player, it will return to us where the player is allowed to place his mark-that is, places where there is not the opponent's mark.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def legal_moves_generator(state_of_current_board,monitor_turn):
    """ Function that returns the set of all possible legal moves and resulting board states.
for a particular input of a board state and player

    Args:
    state_of_current_board: The current board state
    monitor_turn: 1 if it's the player who places the mark 1's turn to play, 0 if it's his opponent's turn

    Returns:
    dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check: A dictionary of a list of possible next coordinate-resulting board state pairs
    The resulting board state is flattened to 1 d array

    """
    dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check={}
    for i in range(state_of_current_board.shape[0]):
        for j in range(state_of_current_board.shape[1]):
            if state_of_current_board[i,j]==2:
                board_state_copy=state_of_current_board.copy()
                board_state_copy[i,j]=monitor_turn
                dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check[(i,j)]=board_state_copy.flatten()
    return dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check
</textarea></div><h2 class="h2">Testing Legal Moves</h2><p>Let's now test the above-created Legal Moves Generator.</p><p>We start a dummy game and flip the toss. We then pass the current board state and the toss winner to the Legal Moves Generator. It returns the dictionary of legal moves that it returns. It's a dictionary of the form "possible next legal move coordinate": "flattened resulting board state".</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">game=game_tic_tac_toe()
game.toss()
print("Player ",game.monitor_turn," has won the toss")
print("Current board state \n",game.board)
dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check=legal_moves_generator(game.board,game.monitor_turn)
print("Dictionary of Possible Next Legal Moves:")
pprint.pprint(dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/ai/images/tic-tac-toe-problem-in-artificial-intelligence3.png"/><p>We can notice above that the returning value of the Legal Moves Generator is a dictionary, containing all the possible legal coordinates and their resulting flattened numpy board states.</p><h2 class="h2">Neural Network Model</h2><p>Here is the model that will do the job, in this the Evaluator function maps board states to scores.</p><img alt="Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/ai/images/tic-tac-toe-problem-in-artificial-intelligence4.png"/><br/><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">model = Sequential()
model.add(Dense(18, input_dim=9,kernel_initializer='normal', activation='relu'))
model.add(Dropout(0.1))
model.add(Dense(9, kernel_initializer='normal',activation='relu'))
model.add(Dropout(0.1))
model.add(Dense(1,kernel_initializer='normal'))

learning_rate = 0.001
momentum = 0.8

sgd = SGD(lr=learning_rate, momentum=momentum,nesterov=False)
model.compile(loss='mean_squared_error', optimizer=sgd)
model.summary()
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/ai/images/tic-tac-toe-problem-in-artificial-intelligence5.png"/><h2 class="h2">Program Move Selector</h2><p>The Program Move Selector picks the next move for a player given the current state of the board in a very orderly fashion. It first relies on the Legal Moves Generator to generate all the possible legal states of the board that can be derived from the current configuration. After having generated the set of next possible board states, it enlists the help of the Evaluator to score each of these states. The scoring is based on several factors; all combined, they evaluate the desirability of each state of the board. The board state that receives the highest score from the evaluator is then chosen for the next move. In this way, it is guaranteed that the move picked will be legal to play and strategic, given the model's criteria of evaluation.</p><img alt="Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/ai/images/tic-tac-toe-problem-in-artificial-intelligence6.png"/><br/><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def move_selector(model,state_of_current_board,monitor_turn):
    """ Function to choose the next move to make from a set of possible legal moves

    Args:
    model: The Evaluator function is used to evaluate each possible next board state
    monitor_turn: 1 if it's the player who places the mark 1's turn to play, 0 if it's his opponent's turn

    Returns:
    selected_move: The numpy array coordinates where the player should place their mark
    new_board_state: The flattened new board state resulting from performing above selected move
    score: The score that was assigned to the above selected_move by the Evaluator (model)

    """
    tracker={}
    dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check=legal_moves_generator(state_of_current_board,monitor_turn)
    for legal_move_coord in dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check:
        score=model.predict(dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check[legal_move_coord].reshape(1,9))
        tracker[legal_move_coord]=score
    selected_move=max(tracker, key=tracker.get)
    new_board_state=dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check[selected_move]
    score=tracker[selected_move]
    return selected_move,new_board_state,score
</textarea></div><h2 class="h2">Testing Program Selector</h2><p>Now, let us simulate a game of Tic-Tac-Toe with the Program Move Selector function choosing the next move. Several moves in a game will be gone through to show how the function evaluates a board status to select an optimal move following the algorithm given.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code"># new game
game=game_tic_tac_toe()
# toss
game.toss()
# choose the first move
print("Player assigned mark 1",game.monitor_turn," won the toss")
print("Initial board state:")
print(game.board)
selected_move,new_board_state,score=move_selector(model,game.board,game.monitor_turn)
print("Selected move: ",selected_move)
print("Resulting new board state: ",new_board_state)
print("Score assigned to above board state by Evaluator(model): ", score)
</textarea></div><p><strong>Output:</strong></p><img alt="Tic-Tac-Toe Problem in Artificial Intelligence" src="https://d2jdgazzki9vjm.cloudfront.net/tutorial/ai/images/tic-tac-toe-problem-in-artificial-intelligence7.png"/><p>Right now, the model evaluator has not been trained yet, hence these weights are random, which makes the above score a random number.</p><h2 class="h2">Opponent's Move</h2><p>What is wrong with Tic-Tac-Toe is the fact that it is too simple, which normally makes the game a repetition. Playing about 10-15 games, we have noticed that the program and its counterpart competitor will engage in a pattern of repetitive moves since they are both following a similar model. It is this lack of variation which is a significant limitation of Tic-Tac-Toe. A game like Checkers, however, would make the learning process much more invigorating due to the additional variability and strategic depth that it brings. In such a setup, for learning, a program would play against a coded opponent developed to simulate various levels of difficulties. This opponent would be coded to change the behavior depending on the mode selected. For instance, in the case of Easy mode, the opponent would result in a very simple approach. On opponents' turns, it generates all the possible legal moves available on the board with the Legal Moves Generator. Then, it randomly picks one of these moves and keeps playing that way until the end of the game. Through this process, the program provides a number of runs without often landing on very hard strategic situations but can examine the space of tic-tac-toe at large.</p><p>However, the opponent in Hard mode adheres to a more strategic methodology. First, it runs a Legal Moves Generator that tells it all the possible legal moves. If any move may result in an immediate win by completing a row, column, or diagonal, then the opponent chooses the move. If the opponent doesn't find a move to win, he would look for moves that would prevent the program from winning on the next turn. That is the defensive strategy of trying to cut the progress of the program. Also, if there is a legal move that produces a configuration of two `0`s in line, column, or diagonal that doesn't include `1`s, the opponent chooses this move. In case none of these are met, it then resorts to picking a move at random. This mixture of offensive and defensive moves makes for a much more realistic and credible opponent for that the program should play, in that the latter must adapt to improve its gameplay.</p><p>To train the program effectively, it plays several times against the coded opponent in either easy or hard modes, with the mode chosen randomly at the beginning of each game. This strategy, or mechanism, of training in two modes is used to balance exploration and strategic learning. There is room for exploration of board states and trying different strategies with less resistance in easy mode. In hard mode, the program is put in a scenario whereby it has to think intensely and play against a more professional opponent. This forms the basis for creating more sophisticated tactics. The program updates the weights of its model at the end of each game. It learns, gradually getting better and improving based on both losses and successes. This iterative process will not only make the program stronger against different opponents and playing situations but will also turn it into an even better player of Tic-Tac-Toe.</p><div class="codeblock"><textarea class="xml" name="code">def row_winning_move_check(state_of_current_board,dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check,monitor_turn):
    """ Function to scan row-wise and find coordinates amongst the legal coordinates that will
Result in a winning state of the board.

    Args:
    dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check: Dictionary of legal next moves
    monitor_turn: whose turn it is to move
    
    Returns:
    selected_move: The coordinates of the numpy array where placing the 0 will lead to a win for the opponent
    """ 
    legal_move_coords =  list(dictionary_of_column_winning_move_check.keys())
    random.shuffle(legal_move_coords)
    for legal_move_coord in legal_move_coords:
        state_of_current_board_copy=state_of_current_board.copy()
        state_of_current_board_copy[legal_move_coord]=monitor_turn
        #check for a win along rows
        for i in range(state_of_current_board_copy.shape[0]):
            if 2 not in state_of_current_board_copy[i,:] and len(set(state_of_current_board_copy[i,:]))==1:
                selected_move=legal_move_coord
                return selected_move
            
